Pulsed high-dose dexamethasone is not 
effective in patients with multifocal motor 
neuropathy 
Leonard H. Van den Berg, MD; Henk Lokhorst, MD; 
John H.J. Wokke, MD 
The hypothesis that multifocal motor neuropathy (MMN) is an 
immune mediated neuropathy has led to the trial of several im- 
munological treatments.’ Prednisolone and plasma exchange 
were ineffective in most patients with MMN. Among the immuno- 
suppressants, only cyclophosphamide given at high doses intrave- 
nously followed by maintenance treatment with oral cyclophos- 
phamide treatment seems to be consistently This 
treatment may have considerable side effects and may be unsuit- 
able for the less severely affected patients. More recently, investi- 
gators have found high dose intravenous immunoglobulin (IvIg) 
treatment to be effective in most patients with MMN,1,4 although 
in many cases frequent IvIg infusions were necessary to maintain 
the effect of treatment.6 In some patients MMN progressed during 
IvIg maintenance the rap^.^ The use of IvIg treatment in MMN 
may be limited by its costs due to the frequency of infusions. 
We tested the efficacy of pulsed, high-dose dexamethasone (40 
mg/d for four consecutive days every 28 days, given in six cycles) 
based on the consideration that pulsed, high-dose dexamethasone 
therapy 1) may be effective in idiopathic thrombocytopenic pur- 
pura (ITP) which is, like MMN, a disease known to respond well to 
IvIg treatment;’ 2) may reduce immunoglobulin production in 
clonal B-cell disorders, even those refractory to other cytotoxic 
agents;7 3) is usually well-tolerated; and 4) is far less expensive 
than IvIg.’ 
Case reports. Two men (27 and 28 years old) were evaluated for 
progressive, asymmetric weakness of arms and legs that had de- 
veloped over a period of 2 and 4 years. Sensation was intact. 
Tendon reflexes were low or absent in affected limbs. Muscle 
strength measurements by hand-hand myometry4 showed in Pa- 
tient l weakness of right abductors of the upper arm (220 Newton 
(N)), left wrist flexors (170 N) and extensors (175 N), and left foot 
flexors (40 N); in Patient 2 weakness of the left wrist flexors (110 
N) and extensors (105 N), right hip flexors (210 N), knee flexors 
(180 N) and foot flexors (130 N). Muscle strength in other muscle 
groups was normal. CSF protein was mildly elevated in both pa- 
tients (5.6 mg/dL in both patients). Electrodiagnostic studies were 
done extensively as described previ~usly.~ Motor conduction blocks 
(outside the sites of nerve compression) were found in Patient 1, in 
the right ulnar and tibia1 nerves; in Patient 2 in the left median 
and ulnar nerves. On the basis of the clinical presentation and 
electrodiagnostic studies, a diagnosis of MMN was made. 
Treatment. In both patients muscle strength improved after 
IVIg treatment (0.4 gkg for 5 days). In Patient 1, muscle strength 
improved in the left foot flexors from 40 to 110 N and to normal 
strength in the other affected muscle groups; in Patient 2, all 
muscle groups returned to normal strength. However, the effect of 
IVIg treatment lasted only 8 weeks in both patients. After IVIg 
was no longer effective and muscle strength had returned to pre- 
treatment levels, both patients were treated with pulsed, high- 
dose dexamethasone (40 mg/d for 4 days every 28 days, given in 
six cycles). Muscle strength was measured before and 1 week after 
onset of treatment, and after that once every 2 weeks using a 
hand-held dynamometer. After 6 months muscle strength had de- 
teriorated in both patients with at least 40 N in all affected mus- 
cle groups. Patient 2 was not able to walk up the stairs without 
support. Weakness was most severe 1 week after medication had 
been taken. Both patients described severe sensations of “pain in 
nerves,” sleeping disturbances, and dysphoria. Both patients re- 
fused further dexamethasone treatment after six cycles and im- 
proved again after another course of IVIg treatment. 
Discussion. We studied whether pulsed, high-dose dexametha- 
sone was effective in patients with MMN who previously had 
responded well to IVIg treatment. A beneficial effect of this treat- 
ment was anticipated considering a potential immunosuppressive 
effect of pulsed, high-dose dexamethasone treatment and the re- 
sponse of immunosuppressive therapy in MMN,3,7 but both pa- 
tients deteriorated during dexamethasone treatment. A steroid- 
induced deterioration was described previously in patients with 
MMN after daily treatment with prednisolone.l,z We cannot rec- 
ommend pulsed, high-dose dexamethasone for patients with 
MMN. 
