Voice fatigue in myasthenia gravis: The 
sinking pitch sign 
Francis 0. Walker, MD 
When myasthenia gravis (MG) affects oral, buccal, lingual, and 
palatal muscles, progressive dysarthria and nasality may result. 
These changes, apparent to the trained ear, can be difficult to 
quantify. We describe a related laryngeal phenomenon that is 
readily elicited, easily measured, and diagnostically helpful. 
Case Report. A 52-year-old woman presented with a six-month 
history of dysphagia and dysarthria that was worse in the 
evening. More recently, she noticed intermittent, horizontal diplo- 
pia. She had particular difficulty singing, unable to sustain either 
clarity or tone. Extensive work-up including endoscopy, barium 
swallow, laryngeal electromyography, and transnasal endoscopic 
laryngoscopy, had all been normal. A Tensilon test, by a neurolo- 
gist, led to a remarkable improvement in her speech. After failing 
to respond to oral anticholinesterase agents, she sought advice 
from our department. 
Examination was remarkable for a flattened smile and nasal, 
dysarthric speech that worsened as she talked. When asked to 
produce a high musical note, the pitch could only be maintained 
for a few seconds before it began to gradually lower. 
Electrodiugnostic studies. Repetitive stimulation studies re- 
vealed baseline decrements of abductor digiti quinti (ADQ) of 4%, 
the nasalis of 24%, and trapezius of 21%, confirming the diagnosis 
of MG. Of particular interest were accelerometer recordings of the 
voice (figure). Asked to produce a single high note, the frequency 
slowly faded, dropping from 400 to 300 Hz over 12 seconds (fig- 
ure); the amplitude of the note also decreased, but to a lesser 
degree. This 25% decrement in frequency correlated well with the 
repetitive stimulation decrements seen in her proximal muscles. 
Discussion. Vocal pitch is primarily determined by the inten- 
sity of contraction of laryngeal muscles. High pitch sounds require 
high tension in both thyroarytenoids and cricothyroids. Lower 
levels of tension in these muscles produce lower pitch. Myasthenic 
fatigue in this patient’s laryngeal muscles, therefore, produced a 
progressively sinking pitch. As neither repetitive stimulation 
studies nor single fiber EMG is practical in these small and sensi- 
tive muscles, measuring pitch decay with an accelerometer is a 
simple, non-invasive, and informative alternative. 
